  abstract: "Public transit networks in secondary and regional agglomerations of the Global South frequently evolve through fragmented commercial permit issuance without formal ridership counts or travel surveys. We present a reproducible open-data framework for evaluating bus route rationalisation, pedestrian catchment accessibility, operational plausibility, and fleet sizing under severe demand-data scarcity, evaluated across the 10 districts of Kashmir Division, India (population 6.58 million). By auditing the operational baseline (Kashmir Valley v3.4.5), we reveal that apparent 71.1% route reductions represent unit changes (collapsing 614 permits into 157 corridors) rather than route withdrawals, that Euclidean buffer catchments overstate walking population served by a third (31.9% network-wide) relative to pedestrian network walksheds, and that operational sanity caps determine headline fleet allocations while understating observed GPS cycle times."
  keywords:
    - "public transit"
    - "bus route rationalisation"
    - "open data"
    - "fleet sizing"
    - "demand-data scarcity"
    - "spatial equity"
    - "walking catchments"
    - "Kashmir Valley"
